  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "LEJ6vuIxf0KL9Rn6wB+awma/Xwg=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:09.400Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "OpenCloud is described as the newer German sovereign file platform, and the research places it in the same shape as Nextcloud: the system produces file events and nothing about the business decisions those files are evidence of.\n\nThe gap is a category difference rather than a missing feature. A file event says a document changed; it does not say that a request was approved, a claim rejected, or an obligation accepted. Sovereign file storage answers where the artefact lives and under whose jurisdiction it sits \u2014 it does not answer who decided what, which is the object public-sector audit asks about.\n\nTiming is what separates this lead from the Nextcloud leads elsewhere in the corpus. Heinlein backs OpenCloud and the platform is new, so the record layer can be designed into it rather than retrofitted around an established interface. Early is cheaper on both sides: fewer compatibility constraints, and a say in where the events are emitted from.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**3 \u00b7 later**\n- Heinlein Support GmbH (DE Berlin) \u2014 https://heinlein-support.de\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> OpenCloud is the newer German sovereign file platform \u2014 same shape as Nextcloud: file events, no business decisions. Heinlein backs it, so this is early enough to be co-designed rather than bolted on.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:28:09.400625Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:09.400631Z"
    }
  },
